  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 245 PM Friday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... The remnants of Isaias will result in unsettling \nweather over the weekend with rainfall Saturday evening and \ncontinuing through Sunday night.\n\nThe remnants of Hurricane Isaias will track through the Tennessee \nValley and transition to extratropical over the weekend and will \nbring deep tropical moisture northeastward toward Central NC. As the \nsystem approaches, some Hi-Res model guidance shows a continuous \nradar reflectivity shield spreading across the region for much of \nthe day Saturday. However, measurable rainfall at the surface will \ninitially be minimal due to a low level dry layer. Model soundings \nshow better saturation above that dry layer in the mid and upper \nlevels, indicating that any early precipitation aloft will evaporate \nas virga well before reaching the ground. Rain falling all the way \nto the ground will not occur until sustained evaporative cooling and \nmoistening fully saturate this sub-cloud layer, with widespread rain \noverspreading the area Saturday evening as strong isentropic lift \ntakes hold.\n\nAs the CAD wedge develops with high pressure centered over New \nEngland, it will keep conditions cool and damp through Saturday \nnight with high temperatures in the low/mid 60s across the NW \nPiedmont to the lower 70s across the SE. The cool wedge acts as a \nstabilizing buffer, suppressing any convective instability and \nkeeping the rainfall rates confined generally to areas across the \nsouth and eastern portions of the forecast area. Models continue to \nshow a brief lull around midday Sunday, with a final broken band of \nshowers and storms moving through during late Sunday afternoon and \nevening. While the severe threat remains low overall due to limited \nCAPE in a somewhat high-shear environment, an isolated strong to \nsevere storm capable of damaging wind gusts cannot be ruled out \nacross the eastern Piedmont, Sandhills, and Coastal Plain if the \nwarm front lifts farther north than currently anticipated at this \ntime. Additionally, a brief tornado is possible as well mainly \nacross the far south and eastern portions of the CWA. \n\nStorm total rainfall across Central NC will average 1 to 2 inches \nthrough late Sunday night. The cold air damming setup will keep \ntotals somewhat lower across the Northwest Piedmont between 0.5 to 1 \ninch, whereas secondary low-pressure development near the coast will \nlead to localized higher amounts of 2-4 inches across the Piedmont \nand Coastal Plain. Although most of Central NC remains in a Marginal \nRisk for flash flooding, the highest relative flooding threats \nremain focused to the west over the mountains and to the east along \nthe coast, though localized urban or poor-drainage flooding remains \nlocal. Precipitation will exit rapidly from west to east overnight \nSunday into early Monday morning as dry high pressure builds in \nbehind the departing system, clearing out clouds and bringing a \nreturn to sunshine by Monday afternoon.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Unseasonably warm, with little to no chance of \nrain, next week\n\nIn the mid/upr-levels, a sub-tropical ridge will develop and \nstrengthen across the w-cntl Gulf Coast through the middle of next \nweek, then progress ewd and across and offshore the Southeast. The \nmodels suggest it will do so downstream of a complex, srn stream-\ndominated trough initially over the Intermountain West, which will \nbecome increasingly influenced by/phased with a nrn stream trough \nforecast to amplify from the Gulf of AK to the upr MS Valley/upr \nGreat Lakes through the same time. Models diverge with the amplitude \nand timing of that trough as it progresses across the ern US next \nweekend, with related decreasing forecast confidence.\n\nAt the surface, high pressure will extend across the Southeast \nthroughout the week. There have been indications in recent days' \nmodel guidance that a moisture-starved backdoor cold front will \nsettle across the Middle Atlantic during the middle of the week, \nthen stall and retreat nwd ahead of a cyclone and frontal system \napproaching with, and related to the aforementioned trough aloft. \nWhile it remains unclear to what extent that backdoor front may \nprogress into cntl NC, any associated influence and post-frontal \ncooling would be favored and mostly confined to Thu. Should that \nboundary remain to the north of cntl NC, it's possible that the \nunseasonably warm pattern that will otherwise extend across the \nSoutheast would support warmer-than-forecast and near record high \ntemperatures in cntl NC mid to late next week.",
